<Base title="How the algorithms are made" description="Where the algorithms on this wiki come from, how moves are counted, how algorithms are ordered and how everything is checked.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How the algorithms are made</h1>
    <p class="lede">The algorithm lists on this wiki were generated by computer search, not copied from other sheets. This page explains how they were found, how moves are counted, which algorithm is listed first, and how everything is checked.</p>

    <h2>How moves are counted</h2>
    <p>Every move count on this wiki is in <b>STM</b> (slice turn metric). Each turn of a face, a slice or a wide layer counts as one move, and half turns count as one too: M, M2, U2, r and R2 are each one move. The turn of the top layer before an algorithm (the pre-AUF) is not counted.</p>
    <p>In HTM, where a slice turn like M counts as two moves, EODFDB takes about 12.5 moves per case instead of 8.0 (centers aligned, top layer lined up first), and 12.7 per position instead of 8.3 (every U turn counted). "Per case" means each case counts once; "per position" weights each case by how often it comes up and counts the turn that lines it up.</p>

    <h2>Cases</h2>
    <p>Each case list is built by listing every position the step can start from, directly from the pieces, then grouping positions that only differ by a turn of the top layer into one case. That's how you know a list is complete. ZBLL comes out at 493 cases, the standard count, and the EODFDB count can be checked by hand with Burnside's lemma, as shown on the <a href="/methods/zbroux/eodfdb/#where-the-numbers-come-from">EODFDB page</a>.</p>

    <h2>EODFDB and EOStripe</h2>
    <p>These steps only use a few move types (M and U for EODFDB; R, r, M and U for EOStripe), so a breadth-first search can visit every position. Every algorithm listed is the shortest possible in STM using those moves. Each EODFDB case is shown from the angle where its shortest solution starts, so no algorithm begins with a turn of the top layer.</p>
    <p>Shortest doesn't always mean nicest to turn. When a case has several shortest solutions, the order is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>EODFDB:</b> fewest M turns (M' and M2 are preferred over M), then fewest half turns.</li>
      <li><b>EOStripe:</b> fewest M turns, then fewest r turns, then fewest half turns, because M is awkward one-handed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nobody has hand-picked these yet, so look at the alternatives listed under each case too.</p>
    <h3>Mirror algorithms</h3>
    <p>The mirror of an EODFDB case is its reflection through the middle slice, left to right. Reflecting a solution turns every U into U' and every U' into U, and leaves M, M' and M2 as they are. Each mirror pair is shown from reflected angles, so the mirror's algorithm is exactly the original with U and U' swapped. There are 992 cases, and one of them is already solved and needs no algorithm, so 991 mirror algorithms are stored. All 991 are checked by the second simulator.</p>
    <p>For LEOR's EOStripe, the table of move counts matches the one RedstoneTim published from a different solver (ksolve++) exactly.</p>

    <h2 id="step-by-step-models">Step-by-step models</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/methods/zbroux/#is-it-worth-it-zbroux-against-roux">Roux comparison</a> models the intuitive steps as they're usually taught. The code is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/tools/stepwise.py`}>stepwise.py</a>. Every position is equally likely, moves are M and U in STM, and the turn of the top layer is counted.</p>
    <p><b>Roux LSE</b>, over all 184,320 positions reachable with M and U from solved (corners at any angle, centers in any of four positions):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>4a</b>: all six edges oriented (each edge's top-or-bottom-color sticker faces up or down), and the top center shows the top or bottom color.</li>
      <li><b>4b</b>: 4a, plus the UL and UR edges solved relative to the corners (after some turn of the top layer, the corners and both edges are in place).</li>
      <li><b>4c</b>: solved, including the final turn of the top layer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>EODFDB</b>, over all 3,840 positions after the second block (top-layer corners ignored), split the same way as LSE:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Step 1</b>: the same goal as 4a. All six edges are oriented, and the top center shows the top or bottom color.</li>
      <li><b>Step 2</b>: both bottom edges on top and opposite each other with their bottom color facing up, edges still oriented, and the top center showing the bottom color. That's 4 positions, one up to turns of the top layer.</li>
      <li><b>Step 3</b>: line them up and do M2 (any shortest finish).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each step is solved with a shortest solution from wherever the previous step left off. When a step has several shortest solutions, the three rules choose differently: <i>lookahead</i> takes the one that makes the remaining steps cheapest; <i>no lookahead</i> treats every shortest move sequence for the step as equally likely; <i>worst choices</i> takes the most expensive. The per-step averages below are measured along the chain, so they add up to the total:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>LSE: 4a + 4b + 4c</th><th>EODFDB: steps 1 + 2 + 3</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Lookahead</td><td>5.95 + 3.63 + 3.26 = 12.84</td><td>5.95 + 2.57 + 1.34 = 9.86</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No lookahead</td><td>5.95 + 4.47 + 5.03 = 15.45</td><td>5.95 + 3.57 + 1.57 = 11.09</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Worst choices</td><td>5.95 + 5.06 + 6.58 = 17.59</td><td>5.95 + 4.26 + 1.83 = 12.04</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>LSE is averaged over all positions with the corners at any angle. Starting with the corners lined up, as after CMLL, gives 12.83, 15.42 and 17.59, so it makes no real difference.</p>
    <p>EODFDB can also be modeled in two steps, merging steps 1 and 2 into one search for the 4 target positions. That gives 7.85 + 1.30 = 9.15, 7.85 + 1.64 = 9.49 and 7.85 + 1.87 = 9.72. It's close to optimal by construction, because the first step aims for a single position, so the Roux comparison uses the three-step split, which matches LSE.</p>
    <p>Step 1 of each method always costs the same under every rule, because only the choice between equally short solutions differs, not their length. "No lookahead" counts move sequences, not end positions. Choosing uniformly between end positions instead gives slightly different numbers.</p>

    <h2 id="zbll">ZBLL</h2>
    <p><b>The ZBLL algorithms are unvetted.</b> They're correct, but no experienced ZBLL solver has chosen them yet, and the first algorithm for a case is often not the one most people use.</p>
    <p>They were found with a meet-in-the-middle search written for this wiki (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/tools/zbll_gen.mts`}>zbll_gen.mts</a>). It searches each of the move sets people use for speed (RU, RUD, RUF, RUL and RUr) separately, up to 16 moves, and keeps every solution it finds. Longer algorithms, or ones that mix more move types, aren't included. Each case lists up to four, ordered by this rule:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>each R or U turn costs 1, r costs 1.15, D, F and L cost 1.5, and B costs 2;</li>
      <li>half turns cost a little extra (0.1 for U2, 0.3 for R2, r2 and D2, 0.4 for F2, L2 and B2);</li>
      <li>a turn of the top layer before the algorithm costs 0.3;</li>
      <li>the lowest total comes first.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A rule like this can't feel finger tricks or regrips, which is why the list needs a human pass.</p>

    <h2>Checking</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every algorithm is applied to its case on a simulated cube and must solve the step.</li>
      <li>EODFDB and EOStripe lists are checked a second time by a separately written simulator, including all 991 EODFDB mirror algorithms.</li>
      <li>ZBLL is checked a second time with <a href="https://github.com/cubing/cubing.js">cubing.js</a>, the open-source library behind alg.cubing.net, which also confirms all 493 cases are different from each other.</li>
      <li>The "paste your scramble" lookups are tested against the same data with hundreds of random positions.</li>
    </ul>

